# sdist contents.
#
# Without this file the sdist file list is setuptools' default, whose only
# tests/ pattern is `tests/test*.py` (see `_add_defaults_optional` in
# setuptools/_distutils/command/sdist.py). That pattern ships the 29
# tests/test_*.py files but matches neither `tests/uptime_kuma_test_case.py` --
# the base class every inherited integration test extends, without which none
# of those 29 files can be imported from the sdist -- nor CHANGELOG.md, which
# `project_urls` advertises.

include CHANGELOG.md
include tests/uptime_kuma_test_case.py

# The test selection config travels with the tests it governs. Without these
# two, `pytest` inside an unpacked sdist would collect the destructive
# integration tests with nothing deselecting them -- the shipped tests would
# behave differently, and more dangerously, than the same tests in the repo.
# Neither matches the `tests/test*.py` default, so both need naming here.
include pytest.ini
include tests/conftest.py

# DO NOT broaden the two lines above into `recursive-include tests ...` or
# `graft tests`.
#
# That same `tests/test*.py` default is the only reason `tests/.env`,
# `tests/.backups/**` (config snapshots containing credentials) and
# `tests/live_test_*.py` are absent from the published sdist: none of those
# names begins with "test". That is a coincidence of spelling, not a policy. A
# broad include here sweeps real credentials into a published artifact, and a
# file published to PyPI cannot be withdrawn.
#
# The exclusions below are the structural backstop for that, and they are NOT
# redundant. `manifest_maker.run` calls `add_defaults()` -- which reads any
# existing `*.egg-info/SOURCES.txt` back into the file list -- BEFORE
# `read_template()` processes this file. So these directives are applied on top
# of whatever a stale SOURCES.txt dragged in, and strip it. Measured: with a
# SOURCES.txt poisoned by a previous `graft tests` build, a build carrying only
# the two `include` lines shipped `tests/.env` and three credential-bearing
# `tests/.backups/config_snapshot_*.json` files; with these lines present, all
# four were gone.
#
# `global-exclude` rather than `exclude` so each pattern holds anywhere in the
# tree -- there is a credential-bearing `.env` at the repository root as well as
# under tests/.
global-exclude .env
global-exclude .live_test_ids.json
global-exclude live_test_*.py
global-exclude *.pyc
prune tests/.backups

# EXPECTED on a clean build: setuptools prints
#   warning: no previously-included files matching '.env' found anywhere in distribution
# once per pattern above. That is the healthy state -- it means the guard found
# nothing to remove. A build where those warnings are ABSENT is one where a
# pattern actually fired, which means something tried to ship a file it should
# not have. Do not "fix" the warnings by deleting the patterns.
#
# Exact sdist contents are enforced separately by `scripts/check_sdist.py`,
# which allowlists tests/ and so also catches residue these patterns do not name
# (stray `__pycache__` entries, `tests/.env.example`). The division is
# deliberate: this file prevents a credential leak structurally, that script
# enforces the precise contents.
